  <ul>
    <li>Confusing divergent and transform boundaries – students often picture lateral motion at divergent margins; remembering that divergence creates new crust helps avoid this error.</li>
    <li>Mixing up magma and lava – the former is subsurface, the latter is erupted; linking the term “lava” to surface processes prevents mis‑labeling.</li>
    <li>Assuming all volcanoes are explosive – overlooking the prevalence of effusive eruptions in shield volcanoes leads to wrong answers; recall the silica‑viscosity relationship.</li>
    <li>Neglecting the age‑progression pattern of hotspot chains – many ignore that older islands lie farther from the current hotspot; visualizing the plate’s motion over a fixed plume clarifies this.</li>
    <li>Over‑generalising rock classifications – treating metamorphic rocks as merely “hard” rocks ignores texture and mineralogy; focusing on protolith and metamorphic grade resolves the confusion.</li>
    <li>Ignoring the role of slab pull in plate motion – many attribute motion solely to mantle convection; remembering slab pull as the dominant force corrects this misconception.</li>
    <li>Misreading diagram scales – especially in cross‑section sketches, students often misinterpret depth vs. horizontal distance; careful attention to scale bars eliminates this pitfall.</li>
  </ul>

