  <p>India’s forest‑wetland matrix is a product of millions of years of geological evolution, climatic oscillations and human stewardship. The earliest recorded reference to Indian forests appears in the <span style="color: var(--warning);">Vedic</span> literature where the term [[Vanaprastha]] denotes a stage of life spent in the woods. By the time of the Mauryan empire, the [[Forest Conservation Act, 1980]] was pre‑figured in Ashoka’s edicts that prohibited indiscriminate tree felling. Understanding this historical backdrop is <span style="color: var(--warning);">important</span> because it shows that forest policy is not a modern invention but a continuum of ecological ethics.</p>

